  "overview": {
    "text": "Saki was the pen name of Hector Hugh Munro (1870–1916), an English writer whose short stories made him known for their sharp wit and satire. Many take aim at upper-class English life, mixing clever humor with a sometimes cruel edge. Munro also wrote journalism, political sketches, a history of Russia, and a novel, but short fiction became his best-known work. Collections such as Reginald, The Chronicles of Clovis, and Beasts and Super-Beasts showcase his unconventional characters, fantastic situations, and knack for an unexpected phrase. His literary signature is the combination of polished social comedy with a distinctly biting, unpredictable tone.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/us/definition/english/saki",
      "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1927_supplement/Munro,_Hector_Hugh",
      "https://orlando.cambridge.org/people/35a25024-886b-4a2d-b1ba-bc01cc110f81"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "Saki was born Hector Hugh Munro in Akyab, Burma, in 1870, and died in France in 1916.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1927_supplement/Munro,_Hector_Hugh",
        "https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/authors/55640/saki/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "He first worked as a political satirist and journalist, and later reported from abroad for the Morning Post.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1927_supplement/Munro,_Hector_Hugh",
        "https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/authors/55640/saki/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "His stories are often set among upper-class English people and use clever, sometimes cruel humor.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/us/definition/english/saki"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Notable story collections include Reginald (1904), The Chronicles of Clovis (1911), and Beasts and Super-Beasts (1914).",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1927_supplement/Munro,_Hector_Hugh",
        "https://orlando.cambridge.org/people/35a25024-886b-4a2d-b1ba-bc01cc110f81"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "His pseudonym, “Saki,” was identified in a 1927 biography as the name of the cupbearer in the Rubáiyát of Omar Khayyám.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1927_supplement/Munro,_Hector_Hugh"
      ]
    }
  ],
